# {{AGENT_NAME}} — Vercel Deploy Agent (operations)

Watches Vercel deployments of this repo and turns failed builds into grounded
GitHub triage issues. Event-driven — no cron, no servers to maintain.
Installed by
[ai-automations](https://github.com/makasanaakshay/ai-automation-master);
re-run its installer to update (answers saved in `automation.config.json`).

```
Vercel deployment.error webhook (hex HMAC-SHA1 verified via x-vercel-signature)
  → Cloudflare Worker relay            scripts/vercel-agent/relay/worker.js
  → repository_dispatch                .github/workflows/vercel-agent.yml
  → agent-run.sh                       restore state → resolve → build-log tail → playbook → save state
```

| Trigger | Playbook | Output (GitHub only — Vercel is never written) |
|---|---|---|
| A deployment ends in ERROR | `analyze` | ONE GitHub issue labeled `{{ISSUE_LABEL}}`: what failed, log excerpt, likely cause (code-verified), where to look, links — or ONE comment when the same commit sha already has an open issue |

State lives on the orphan branch `vercel-agent-state`
(`state/<deployment_id>.json`: phase, gh_issue_url, commit_sha). Every run
re-fetches truth from Vercel's API and diffs against it — webhook payloads are
only doorbells. Dedupe is **sha-search based**, not state-keyed: state only
prevents reprocessing the same deployment (retriggers, duplicate deliveries);
a NEW deployment of an already-failed commit is caught by the playbook
searching open `{{ISSUE_LABEL}}` issues for the commit sha and commenting
instead of filing. The build log's tail (~8000 chars) is fetched up front and
inlined into the prompt, so triage works even when the failure predates the
run. Issue bodies and comments end with the `{{AGENT_MARKER}}` footer.

## Operating

- **Recover / backfill:** Actions → *Vercel Deploy Agent* → *Run workflow* — empty `item_id` reconciles the 10 most recent failed deployments (`state=ERROR`, scoped to the configured project when set) + tracked ones; a deployment id processes one.
- **Transcripts:** every run uploads `.agent-out/` as an artifact (prompt, build-log context, stream).
- **Model:** `AI_MODEL` in `scripts/vercel-agent/env.sh` (currently `{{AI_MODEL}}`, brain `{{BRAIN_NAME}}`).
- **Pause:** delete (or disable) the webhook in Vercel Team/Account Settings → Webhooks — the relay can stay deployed; recreating the webhook mints a new secret (`wrangler secret put VERCEL_WEBHOOK_SECRET` again).
- **Known blind spot:** deployments that fail while the webhook is down are only caught by the reconcile button — it scans the last 10 failed deployments.

## Local dry-run (safe — needs VERCEL_TOKEN exported)

```bash
DRY_RUN=1 bash scripts/vercel-agent/agent-run.sh                 # reconcile scan
DRY_RUN=1 ITEM_ID=<deployment_id> bash scripts/vercel-agent/agent-run.sh
```

## Guardrails

- ONE GitHub issue per failing commit sha — new failed deployments of the same commit become comments on it; closed issues are left closed.
- Vercel access is READ-ONLY — the agent never retries, cancels, redeploys, or promotes a deployment; humans own the deploy pipeline.
- No secrets from build logs: env values, tokens, and connection strings that leak into logs are scrubbed before anything reaches GitHub.
- Never writes code — git stays clean apart from the state branch.
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` only inside the disposable CI runner; local runs are `DRY_RUN` and stop before any AI call.
